    body: `
<p>So far every consonant had a vowel after it. Real words also put two consonants together, and Telugu has a neat way to write that. This is the last script lesson.</p>
<p>The romanisation is a crutch: read the Telugu first and glance down only to check.</p>
<h3>The small letter underneath: vattu</h3>
<p>When two consonants meet with no vowel between them, the second one is written small, under or beside the first. This small form is called a vattu. Read the big letter first, then the vattu, then the vowel sign. In <b>పుస్తకం</b> <i>pustakam</i> the small త under స gives st.</p>
<h3>Doubled consonants</h3>
<p>When the vattu is the same letter, the consonant is doubled: hold it, as in English <i>bookcase</i> or <i>midday</i>. A doubled consonant changes the word.</p>
{{ex:అమ్మ|amma|mother}}
{{ex:అక్క|akka|older sister}}
{{ex:గది|gadi|room}}
{{ex:గద్ది|gaddi|throne}}
<p>Some vattu forms look nothing like the full letter. The commonest is ర, which becomes a small hook: <b>ప్రేమ</b> <i>prēma</i> "love", <b>శ్రీ</b> <i>shrī</i> (a title, like Mr., before a man's name). క and ష together make <b>క్ష</b> <i>ksha</i>, as in <b>క్షమించండి</b> <i>kshaminchaṇḍi</i> "sorry; excuse me".</p>
<h3>Sunna: one circle, three sounds</h3>
<p>The small circle ం is the sunna, a nasal sound. What it sounds like depends on the next letter. Your mouth is already getting ready for that letter, so the nasal takes its place.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Next letter</th><th>Sunna said</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>k, ch or t row</td><td>n</td><td class="k">అంత (that much), మంచి (good)</td></tr><tr><td>ṭ row</td><td>ṇ</td><td class="k">పండు (fruit)</td></tr><tr><td>p row</td><td>m</td><td class="k">అంబ (mother: a poetic word)</td></tr><tr><td>end of word, or య ర ల వ శ ష స హ</td><td>m</td><td class="k">పుస్తకం (book)</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:పండు|paṇḍu|fruit}}
{{ex:అంత|anta|that much}}
{{ex:పుస్తకం|pustakam|book}}
<h3>Pollu: no vowel at all</h3>
<p>The mark ్ on top is the pollu. It removes the built-in a, so the consonant is said with no vowel. You see it mostly at the end of English loans.</p>
{{ex:బస్|bas|bus}}
{{ex:డాక్టర్|ḍākṭar|doctor}}
<p>The sign ః (visarga, said h) turns up in a few Sanskrit words; recognise it and move on.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> saying a doubled consonant as a single one. <b>అమ</b> → <b>అమ్మ</b> <i>amma</i>, "mother": hold the m.</div>
`,
